A handler that reads the loop variable of the loop its node is in. A promoted
top-level handler cannot see one -- it is written outside the loop -- so the
handler is attached as a closure instead, which captures it.

Unrolling used to hide this: a view loop over a constant list was unrolled for
every target and each handler got the element substituted in as a literal,
capturing nothing. Once a target with a host language emitted the loop, the
promoted handler referenced a variable that was not in scope and
examples/calculator stopped building on fyne. Both targets here show the same
shape: the host's own loop over m.items, a closure attached inside it --
OnTapped on fyne, gtk4rt.Connect on gtk4 -- and a body that reads `it`
directly.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `__n1_click_handler` -- the handler is a closure in the loop, not a promoted top-level method that could not see `it`
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `func\(\) any` -- the closure returns nothing, so it assigns to a func() field; `func() any` was rejected by Go twice over
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `\t*__n0.Text =` -- the closure body goes through the platform translator, so the label write is fyne's setter and not a raw IR assignment to a bare name
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `_clicked_handler` -- gtk4 attaches the same closure through gtk4rt.Connect rather than promoting a named handler

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

component main {
    var items list<string> = ["a", "b"]
    var picked = ""

    vbox {
        for var it = items {
            button(text=it, @click { picked = it })
        }
    }
    text(value=picked)
}
